//! check: fail(E0401)
//! phase: resolve
//! conforms: type.fn.ret, gram.expr.block, gram.expr.tagident
//!
//! wolf-lang#284 witness 2 (from wolf-interp is43, #73): a `()` tail
//! under `-> !int`. `[type.fn.ret]` checks a fallible body's tail
//! against the ok half of its declared row exactly as an infallible
//! one — a `print` statement is `()`, not an `int`, and the missing
//! status is a mismatch at the tail, never a status invented by the
//! runtime (lupin 0.1.30 printed `hi` and exited 0 — wolf-interp#69's
//! shape one level up). The compiler answers E0401 at `print(s)`,
//! naming the declared return type as the origin.

fn main() -> !int {
    let s = "hi"
    print(s)
}
